                        %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^n A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            could be more general than an integer; see below.
          %p
            For suitable choices of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            taught in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+cohomology"} sheaf cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercohomology"} hypercohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
          %p
            Furthermore, this general notion of cohomology also accurately captures general classification and extension problems (
            %a{:href => "#NSS"}> NSS
            ), such as (1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extensions"} group extensions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} fiber ∞-bundles
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} twisted ∞-bundles
          %p A non-technical introduction to some concepts in cohomology from this perspective is at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"}> motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
              \.
          %p The following section
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Overview"} Overview
          %p gives a tour through the zoo of cohomology theories traditionally known, indicating how they all fit into this picture. Then the section
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Definition"} Definition
          %p
            gives the general formal definition and discusses general properties of and constructions in cohomology theory, such as the terminology of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            and
            %em coboundaries
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} objects classified
            by cohomology, of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+classes"} characteristic classes
            of these objects, of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+tower+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} Postnikov towers
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} Whitehead towers
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ExtraStruc"} Extra structure on cohomology
          %p
            describes additional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} stuff, structure, property
            that may be present for certain choices of coefficient objects – such as
            %em gradings
            ,
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            %em required
            (differently) on cohomology.
          %p
            The general definition of cohomology in terms of mapping spaces in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            also encompasses notions that can be considered
            %em variants
            of “honest” cohomology, notably that of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            (which includes other cases such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            (with its different flavors such as Borel-equivariant and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bredon+cohomology"} Bredon cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Variants"} Variants
          %p before the next main section
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
          %p then starts going through concrete examples in detail. The reader uneasy with the abstract generality of our perspective is advised to skip ahead to this section and find from a long list of examples discussed his or her favorite traditional notion of cohomology and how it fits into the general structure.
          %p
            Finally we discuss why
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            are a particularly nice environment for cohomology in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ToposTheory"} Cohomology in (∞,1)-topoi
              \.
          %p Essentially nothing about this perspective on cohomology is really new, many aspects of it have been made explicit in the literature here and there. In fact, to some extent everything here is just an afterthought of the old seminal article
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"}> Kenneth Brown
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} Abstract homotopy theory and generalized sheaf cohomology
              (1973),
          %p
            in the light of fully fledged
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} homotopy categories
            of
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercomplete+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} hypercomplete (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} 1-categorical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            as reviewed for instance in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              %em Classifying Spaces and Classifying Topoi
              , section I.4
          %p
            by noticing that the constructions on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            used there secretly precisely compute the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+hom-space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom-objects
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            as presented by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sheaves"} model structure on simplicial sheaves
            on the underlying site.
          %p This and a list of other related references and historical developments is given at
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#References"}> References
              \.
          %h3#ToposTheoryHistoricalAspects About the nPOV on cohomology
          %p
            As we will see in the list of examples below, large numbers of examples of notions of cohomology do happen to have a natural interpretation in terms of connected components of hom-spaces in
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em wrong
            definitions, as illustrated by the following example.
          %p
            In the literature there is a
            %em naive
            definition of Lie group cohomology and topological group cohomology, which is not interpretable in terms of hom-spaces in any natural
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
          %p …
          %h2#Overview Tour through notions of cohomology
          %p
            The statement of the above slogan is well familiar for the special case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \coloneqq K(\mathbb{Z}, n)
            an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            any topological space that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X,K(\mathbb{Z},n)) = H^n(X,\mathbb{Z})
          %p
            coincides with the “ordinary”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
          %p
            This definition in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            alone already goes a long way. By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} Brown representability theorem
            all cohomology theories that are called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theories are of this form, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a topological space that is part of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+cohomology+theory"} complex cobordism
          %p
            Another big complex of notions of cohomology that on first sight maybe does not seem to fit into this pattern is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categorical+hom-space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom-space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}(X,A)
            in cases where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presented
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
          %p
            Indeed, it turns out that an old result from the 1960s,
            %strong Verdier’s hypercovering theorem
            effectively shows that what was introduced as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            is really nothing but an instance of the above general setup. A particularly clear-sighted understanding of this fact was presented in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"}> Kenneth Brown
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} Abstract homotopy theory and generalized sheaf cohomology
          %p
            Therein Brown considers essentially the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
            – which today is known to be one of the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models+for+%E2%88%9E-stack+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} models for ∞-stack (∞,1)-toposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            – rederives Verdier’s hypercovering theorem and shows that ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            is indeed nothing but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X,A)
            in such an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaf"} simplicial presheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            happens to be objectwise in the image of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %em maximally abelian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
          %p
            One can then understand various “cohomology theories” as nothing but tools for
            %em computing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X,A)
            using the known presentations of
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categorical+hom-space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom-space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+cohomology"} Čech cohomology
            computes these spaces by finding cofibrant models for the domain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+nerve"} Čech nerve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            find fibrant models for the codomain
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            In other words,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            is of the exact same nature as the familiar cohomology of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            to a more general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} ∞-stack (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+cohomology"} deRham cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+cohomology"} etale cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystalline+cohomology"} crystalline cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntomic+cohomology"} syntomic cohomology
          %p
            But this also shows that abelian sheaf cohomology itself is just a very special case of cohomology in an
            = succeed "-stack" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-topos:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable
            or
            %em maximally abelian
            case. For coefficient objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathbf{H}
            that are not maximally abelian (for instance not degreewise in the image of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            for sheaf cohomology) the cohomology of an
            = succeed "-stack" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
          %p
            Often in the literature the term “nonabelian cohomology” is restricted to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+group+cohomology"} nonabelian group cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            with coefficients in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}G
            of a (possibly nonabelian)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            (which is of course not part of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
          %p
            If the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            here is generalized to a (possibly nonabelian)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}G
            gives degree 2 nonabelian cohomology in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
            %em Cohomologie non abélienne
            is concerned with this case. In fact, Giraud considered
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-topos"} 2-topos
            with both the domain and the coefficient object allowed to have nontrivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group+%28of+an+infinity-stack%29"} homotopy groups of stacks
            in degree 2.
          %p
            Conceptually, with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            in hand, there is no problem in generalizing nonabelian cohomology and its relation to gerbes and principal bundles further from stacks to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin structure
            on a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            requires a 1-stack coefficient object and classifies principal bundles, and the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+structure"} string structure
            requires a 2-stack coefficient object and classifies
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+group"} fivebrane structure
            requires 6-stack coefficient objects and classifies principal 6-bundles. Generally, we may speak of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to A
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            Various other notions of cohomology are special cases of this. For instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
            is nothing but the cohomology in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            on objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbf{B}G
            that are
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+group+cohomology"} nonabelian group cohomology
            is nothing but the general case of this where there is no restriction on the coefficient object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty Grpd
            – which is the
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-stack" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Sh
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Diff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} = Sh_{(\infty,1)}(Diff)
            are
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-groupoid
            %em smooth group cohomology
            \. In this context for instance one can give cohomological interpretations of smooth realizations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+2-group"} string 2-group
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fivebrane+6-group"} fivebrane 6-group
          %p
            Conversely, given an unconstrained (unstable)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            with its general notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable
            or
            %em abelian
            content by considering objects that are components of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+object"} spectrum object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            one further considers the cohomology of spectrum objects themselves, which is an example of the notion of cohomology being used in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            which is not an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-spaces"} pro-spaces
            or more generally of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-objects"} pro-objects
            in an (∞,1)-topos, which is important in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape theory
          %p
            There are some slight variations on the theme that cohomology is all about connected components of hom-spaces in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+hom+space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom-spaces
            instead, one finds
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} slice (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi PU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mn 2
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top(-,\mathcal{B}PU(n)) \to Top(-,\mathcal{B}^2 U(1))
            that sends a projective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} unitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            (hence its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+gerbe"} lifting gerbe
            for the lift of its structure group to the full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} unitary group
          %p
            Another example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theories with coefficient objects a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is the study of the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ch
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi dR
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ch : \mathbf{H}(X,E) \to \Omega^\bullet_{dR}(X)\otimes \pi_\bullet(E)
            from
            = succeed "-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+cohomology"} deRham cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            on the underlying bundles, for instance
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simons-Sullivan+structured+bundle"} Simons-Sullivan structured bundle
          %p
            By generalizing the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            to richer
            = succeed "-toposes," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} differential cohomology in an (∞,1)-topos
            encoding connections on general
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-vector+bundle"} ∞-vector bundle
          %p
            %br/
          #Slogan.standout
            %p
              %strong Slogan.
            %p
              Thousand and one definitions of notions of cohomology and its variants. From the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+hom+space"} ∞-categorical hom-space
              in an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p We give now the very general definition of cohomology and describe very general properties of and very general constructions in cohomology theory.
          %h3#general_definition General definition
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            we have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+hom+space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom-space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}(X,A)
            – an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Ho
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = Ho_{\mathbf{H}}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} homotopy category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Ho
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X,A) = Ho_{\mathbf{H}}(X,A)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c : X \to A) \in \mathbf{H}(X,A)
                are called
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"}> cocycle
                  s
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                with coefficients in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is understood to be equipped with the structure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*} \to A
                of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mo *
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to {*} \to A
                is the
                %strong trivial cocycle
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mi triv
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{triv}
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda : c_1 \to c_2
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}(X,A)
                are the
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong coboundaries
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong cohomologous
            %li
              %p
                the equivalence classes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [c] \in \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X,A)
                of cohomologous are the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %strong cohomology classes
            %li
              %p
                the set of cohomology classes is the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %strong cohomology set
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≔
                      %msub
                        %mi Ho
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      H(X,A) \coloneqq Ho_{\mathbf{H}}(X,A) = \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X,A)
              %p
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in \mathbf{H}(X,A)
                a cocycle on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbf{H}(A,B)
                a cocycle on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi c
                      %mi A
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi k
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      [k(c)] \coloneqq [X \stackrel{c}{\to} A \stackrel{k}{\to} B] \in H(X,B)
              %p
                is the
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} characteristic class
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                with respect to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            %strong Remark
            Notice that there is
            %strong no notion of cochain
            in this general setup. What are called
            %em cochains
            are specifically components of certain specific
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} models
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}(X,A)
            %a{:href => "#abelian"} abelian cohomology
            below.
          %h3#objects_classified_by_cohomology Objects classified by cohomology
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : X \to A
            a cocycle, one says that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            is the object
            %strong
              classified by the cohomology class
              %em .
          %p
            In an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundles"} principal bundles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbes"} gerbes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundles"} principal 2-bundles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} group object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+extension"} group extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}(X,A)
            is often denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext(X,A)
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            is then called an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ext+functor"} Ext
          %h3#characteristic_classes Characteristic classes
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathbf{H}
            some coefficient object and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{c_n : A \to E_n\}
            a collection of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n \in \mathbf{H}
            – typically chosen to be
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+object"} Eilenberg-MacLane object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            induces a map of cohomology
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c_n : \mathbf{H}(X,A) \to \mathbf{H}(X,E_n)
          %p and hence of cohomology classes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c_n : H(X,A) \to H(X,E_n)
          %p
            that sends each
            = succeed "-cocycle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            to its
            %strong characteristic class
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_n(g)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            classified by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_n(P)
            of this principal
            = succeed "-bundle." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h3#ExtraStruc Extra structure on cohomology
          %p
            Extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} stuff, structure, property
            on the coefficient object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            will induce corresponding stuff, structure or property on the cohomology sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X,A)
          %h4#Grading Gradings
          %h5#integer_grading Integer grading
          %p
            In the case that the coefficient object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            admits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n \in \mathbb{N})
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^n A
            one writes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^n(X,A) \coloneqq \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X, \mathbf{B}^n A)
          %p
            and speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                \-cohomology in degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Similarly,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} looping
            defines negative degree cohomology:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^{-n}(X,A) \coloneqq \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X, \Omega^n A)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Because
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+hom+space"} (∞,1)-categorical hom
            – as any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            – preserves
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi H
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 0
                          %msup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi n
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mi n
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi H
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo .
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  H^{-n}(X,A) &amp;\simeq \pi_0 \Omega^n \mathbf{H}(X, A)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \simeq \pi_n \mathbf{H}(X,A)
                  \\,.
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This means that all the non-positive degree cohomology identifies with the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}(X,A)
          %h5#exotic_gradings Exotic gradings
          %p
            In many cases, the (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            is related to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
            via a symmetric monoidal adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo ⇆
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^\infty: \mathbf{H} \leftrightarrows \mathbf{S}: \Omega^\infty
          %p
            which is usually some form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            with coefficients in objects of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\infty A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+group"} Picard group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Pic
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pic(\mathbf{S})
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Pic
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\star(X, A)=\pi_0\mathbf{S}(X,\star\otimes A), \quad \star\in Pic(\mathbf{S}).
          %p The point of the Picard-grading is that it accounts for all possible suspension isomorphisms.
          %p
            For example, there is always the (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mi Stab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}=Stab(\mathbf{H})
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+objects"} spectrum objects
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi Pic
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\subset Pic(\mathbf{S})
            consisting of the spheres
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n \coloneqq \Sigma^n(1)
            gives the integer grading discussed above in the special case when the coefficient object is a spectrum object. This is discussed further
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#abelian"} below
          %p
            Examples where some subgroup of the Picard group larger than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            is commonly used include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
                is the (∞,1)-category of
                = succeed "-spaces" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi RO
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RO(G)
                which is a subgroup of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Pic
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Pic(\mathbf{S})
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}=\infty Grpd/X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi S
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi E
                      %mi Mod
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}=[X,E Mod]
                for some
                = succeed "-ring" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mo ⋆
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\star(1,E)
                is the same as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted
                = succeed "-cohomology" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi Grpd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}=\infty Grpd
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
                is the
                = succeed "-local" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(n)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                and some
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\geq 1
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+integers"} p-adic integers
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_p
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+homotopy+category"} motivic homotopy category
                over a base scheme
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{S}
                is the associated stable motivic homotopy category. The Picard group contains a copy of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\times K_0(S)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}
                (with a re-indexing). This recovers for example the bigrading in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic cohomology
          %h4#abelian Abelian and stable cohomology
          %p
            Often the coefficient object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathbf{H}
            for cohomology is taken to be indefinitely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} deloopable
            – an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
            – or, more generally, a component of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+object"} spectrum object
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Stab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Stab(\mathbf{H})
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
          %p
            In terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Σ
                          %mn ∞
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mn ∞
                  %mi Stab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{H} \stackrel{\stackrel{\Omega^\infty}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{\Sigma^\infty}{\to}}
                  Stab(\mathbf{H})
          %p
            this means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A = E_n \coloneqq \Omega^\infty \circ \Sigma^n E
          %p
            for some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+object"} spectrum object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (not necessarily a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
          %p
            One single such spectrum object this way yields a
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^n(X, E) \coloneqq \pi_0 \mathbf{H}(X, \Omega^\infty \Sigma^n E)
          %p
            which taken together, denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\bullet(X,E)
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            this special case of cohomology is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
          %h4#CohomGroup Cohomology groups and rings
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            happens to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} group object
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            then the cohomology
            %em set
            naturally inherits the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            and then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ;
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X;A)
            is called the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is at least an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_2
            object, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ;
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X;A)
            is abelian.
          %p
            This is in particular necessarily the case if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a component of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+object"} spectrum object
            in abelian cohomology in the sense described above, i.e. of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn ∞
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi n
                    %mi A
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\infty \Sigma^n A'
          %p
            If the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+object"} spectrum object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
            in addition carries the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            — in which case it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectrum"} ring spectrum
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            — then we speak of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+cohomology+theory"} multiplicative cohomology theory
            and the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^\bullet(X,A)
            form a graded
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %strong cohomology ring
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with coefficients in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#Variants Variants
          %h4#twisted_cohomology Twisted cohomology
          %p
            What is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            is just the intrinsic cohomology of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+toposes"} slice toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theories"} generalized cohomology theories
            then this is the intrinsic cohomology of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} tangent (∞,1)-toposes
          %h4#differential_cohomology Differential cohomology
          %p
            A special type of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} characteristic class
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            with respect to the Chern character is
            = succeed "." do
              %strong differential cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential abelian cohomology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} differential cohomology in an (∞,1)-topos
          %h4#equivariant_cohomology Equivariant cohomology
          %p
            All flavors of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            are obtained from the cohomology of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} slice (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}_{\mathbf{B}G}
            (which encapsulates
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-actions"} ∞-actions
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
                in terms of
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+theory"} (∞,1)-topos theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles+--+theory%2C+presentations+and+applications"}> FSS 12 I, exmp. 4.4
              ):
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+type"} pointed
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+homotopy+type"} connected
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
