                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            \-Sheaf-cohomology /
            = succeed "-stack-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            A Grothendieck–Rezk–Lurie
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-sheaves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
            or
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+stack"} derived stack
          %h4#abelian_sheaf_cohomology Abelian sheaf cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} Abelian sheaf cohomology
                for complexes of sheaves in non-negative degree is cohomology of the sub-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
                of
                = succeed "-stacks" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
                which, under the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
                come from
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} Abelian sheaf cohomology
                for unbounded complexes of sheaves is stable cohomology of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} stable (∞,1)-topos
                of
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-sheaves
          %p
            Several familiar “cohomology theories” are not so much genuine cohomology theories as rather computational techniques for computing certain cohomology classes in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            by using 1-categorical tools of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+category+theory"} homotopy coherent category theory
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+categories"} derived categories
            and the like.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+cohomology"} Čech cohomology
                is the technique of computing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X,A)
                by computing 1-categorical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-sets"} hom-sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\hat X,A)
                on
                %em resolutions
                of the domain object
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                The technique of computing
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                by computing the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  derived global section functor
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/derived+global+section+functor"} ?
                is similarly a technique of computing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(X,A)
                in terms of 1-categorical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-sets"} hom-sets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mover
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(X,\hat A)
                into
                %em resolutions
                of the coefficient object (namely
                = succeed "s)." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolution"} injective resolution
          .query
            %p Zoran: I am not happy with this assertion. First of all the notion of the derived functor is fundamental and it makes sense even in setups when the injective resolutions do not exist. Abelian sheaf cohomology IS a derived functor of the global sections functor, not a specific technique to computing it. On the other hand, the injective resolutions ARE a specific technique to compute the derived functor. It is also not clear in this entry if it is about sheaves on topological spaces or on sites or some more general setup.
            %p
              Urs: I have posted a reply
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntnu.no/~stacey/Mathforge/nForum/comments.php?DiscussionID=391&page=1#Item_5"} here
          %h4#NonabelianSheafCohomology Nonabelian sheaf cohomology with constant coefficients
          %p
