              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
              in the form of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cellular+cohomology"} cellular cohomology
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrei+Kolmogoroff"}> Andrei Kolmogoroff
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Über die Dualität im Aufbau der kombinatorischen Topologie
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://mi.mathnet.ru/msb5361"} mathnet
            %p
              A footnote on the first page reads as follows, giving attribution to
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Alexander35a"} Alexander 35a
              = succeed ":" do
                %a{:href => "#Alexander35a"} 35b
            %blockquote
              %p
                Die Resultate dieser Arbeit wurden für den Fall gewöhnlicher Komplexe vom Verfasser im Frühling und im Sommer 1934 erhalten und teilweise an der Internationalen Konferenz für Tensoranalysis (Moskau) im Mai 1934 vorgetragen. Die hier dargestellte allgemeinere Theorie bildete den Gegenstand eines Vortrages, den der Verfasser an der Internationalen Topologischen Konferenz (Moskau, September 1935) hielt; bei letzterer Gelegenheit erfuhr er, dass ein grosser Teil dieser Resultate im Falle von Komplexen indessen von Herrn Alexander erhalten worden ist. Vgl. die inzwischen erschienenen Noten von Herrn
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+W.+Alexander"} Alexander
                in den «Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences U.S.A.», 21, (1935), 509—512. Herr Alexander trug über seine Resultate ebenfalls an der Moskauer Topologischen Konferenz vor. Verallgemeinerungen für abgeschlossene Mengen und die Konstruktion eines Homologieringes für Komplexe und abgeschlossene Mengen, über welche der Verfasser ebenso an der Tensorkonferenz 1934 vorgetragen hat, werden in einer weiteren Publikation dargestellt. Diese weitere Begriffsbildungen sind übrigens ebenfalls von Herrn Alexander gefunden und teilweise in den erwähnten Noten publiziert.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrei+Kolmogoroff"}> Andrei Kolmogoroff
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Homologiering des Komplexes und des lokal-bicompakten Raumes
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "http://mi.mathnet.ru/msb5475"} mathnet
              %li#Alexander35a
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+W.+Alexander"}> J. W. Alexander
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em On the chains of a complex and their duals
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.21.8.50"} doi:10.1073/pnas.21.8.50
              %li#Alexander35b
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+W.+Alexander"}> J. W. Alexander
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em On the ring of a compact metric space
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.21.8.511"} doi:10.1073/pnas.21.8.511
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+W.+Alexander"}> J. W. Alexander
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em On the connectivity ring of an abstract space
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1968484"} doi:10.2307/1968484
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/alexcon.pdf"} pdf
            %p
              The term “cohomology” was introduced by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hassler+Whitney"} Hassler Whitney
              in
            %ul
              %li#Whitney37
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hassler+Whitney"}> Hassler Whitney
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em On matrices of integers and combinatorial topology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/duke-mathematical-journal/volume-3/issue-1/On-matrices-of-integers-and-combinatorial-topology/10.1215/S0012-7094-37-00304-1.short"} doi:10.1215/S0012-7094-37-00304-1
            %p See also
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hassler+Whitney"}> Hassler Whitney
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On products in a complex
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1968795"} doi:10.2307/1968795
            %p
              The notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
              is due to
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Singular homology theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/1969185"} doi:10.2307/1969185
            %p
              The notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+cohomology"} monadic cohomology
              via
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+resolutions"} canonical resolutions
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jon+Beck"} Jon Beck
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Homology and Standard Constructions
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seminar+on+Triples+and+Categorical+Homology+Theory"} Seminar on Triples and Categorical Homology Theory
                  = succeed "," do
                    %strong 80
                  %strong 18
                  (2008) 186-248 [
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/18/tr18abs.html"}> tac:18
                  ,
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/18/tr18.pdf"} pdf
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Cartan-Eilenberg cohomology and triples
                  %strong 112
                  3 (1996) 219–238 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049(95)00138-7"}> doi:10.1016/0022-4049(95)00138-7
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://www.math.mcgill.ca/barr/papers/coho.pdf"} pdf
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Barr-CECohomology.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Algebraic cohomology: the early days
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Galois Theory, Hopf Algebras, and Semiabelian Categories
                  %strong 43
                  (2004) 1–26 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/fic/043"}> doi:10.1090/fic/043
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "https://www.math.mcgill.ca/barr/papers/algcohom.pdf"} pdf
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Barr-AlgebraicCohomology.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %p
              The general abstract perspective on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
              (subsuming
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+cohomology"} sheaf cohomology
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercohomology"} hypercohomology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
              and indications of
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"}> Kenneth Brown
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} Abstract homotopy theory and generalized sheaf cohomology
                (1973)
          %p For more on the pre-history of the notion of cohomology see
          %ul
            %li
              MO entry,
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/39597/timeline-of-cohomology-1935-to-1938"} Timeline of cohomology (1935 to 1938)
          %p A bunch of survey information on types of cohomoloy theories is kept here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Holmstrom"}> Andreas Holmstrom
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomology+Theory+Database"} Cohomology Theory Database
          %h3#general General
          %p The general abstract perspective on cohomology as highlighted in the text above was essentially established in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"}> Kenneth Brown
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} Abstract homotopy theory and generalized sheaf cohomology
              (1973)
          %p but probably known in one form or other before that.
          %p This article establishes that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                all of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
              %ul
                %li
                  with all its special cases like, say,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            %li
              %p
                all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
              %ul
                %li
                  with simple things like
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} ordinary cohomology
                  and more intricate things like
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+spectrum"} K-theory
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            %li
              %p
                as well as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
              %ul
                %li
                  classifying
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbes"} gerbes
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
            %li
              %p
                as well as the more mundane special cases of this like
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
                and, yes, cohomology of cochain complexes itself
          %p are naturally special cases of one single concept: that of hom-sets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Ho
                    %mi SSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H(X,A)
                  \\coloneqq
                  Ho_{SSh}(X,A)
          %p
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
          %p The only fundamental new addition to this insight that is available now and was not available in 1973 is that
          %ul
            %li
              These categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Ho
                      %mi SSh
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = Ho_{SSh}
              are precisely the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} hom-wise decategorification
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-sheaves
              otherwise known as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topoi"} (∞,1)-topoi
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
          %p
            This is propositon 6.5.2.1 in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
            and builds on the fundamental work by K. Brown, Joyal and Jardine and others on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
          %p
            For a
            %em motivation
            of these definitions from the point of view of cohomology as a homotopy hom-set of
            = succeed "-stacks" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Dugger"}> Dan Dugger
              ,
              %em Sheaves and homotopy theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.uoregon.edu/~ddugger/cech.html"}> web
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/cech.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The general abstract picture of cohomology as connected components of mapping spaces in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            is the topic of section 7.2.2 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
          %p
            Notice that the discussion there is, as often in the literature, given from the perspective of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/petit+topos"} petit topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒳
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{X}
            as that of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (instead of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros topos
            of
            %em all
            generalized spaces, as we do in the above entry). Accordingly then from that perspective one wants to study the cohomology of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself, which corresponds to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            in the
            = succeed "-topos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+object"} Eilenberg-MacLane object
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(A,n)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒳
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mi 𝒳
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo ,
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^n(\mathcal{X},A)
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\pi_0\mathcal{X}({*}, K(A,n))
          %p (definition 7.2.2.14).
          %p
            A comprehensive account of the full non-abelian case and its classification of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} principal ∞-bundles
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-gerbes"} ∞-gerbes
            and the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            is in
          %ul
            %li#NSS
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Nikolaus"}> Thomas Nikolaus
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danny+Stevenson"} Danny Stevenson
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles+--+theory%2C+presentations+and+applications"} Principal ∞-bundles – theory, presentations and applications
              (2012)
          %p
            with generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Proper+Orbifold+Cohomology"} Proper Orbifold Cohomology
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.01101"}> arXiv:2008.01101
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            and with discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character"} Chern-Dold character
            on cohomology understood in this generality:
          %ul
            %li#FSS20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"}> Domenico Fiorenza
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/The+Character+Map+in+Twisted+Non-Abelian+Cohomology"} The Character Map in Nonabelian Cohomology — Twisted, Differential, Generalized
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.11909"} arXiv:2009.11909
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/13422"} doi:10.1142/13422
          %p
            Another reference with a discussion of cohomology in the general sense discussed above, using tools of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            theory for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
          %ul
            %li
              Brian Conrad,
              %em Cohomological descent
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.stanford.edu/~conrad/papers/hypercover.pdf"}> pdf
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
            Last revised on June 11, 2025 at 19:37:54.
